Here's a focused plan and minimal patch to fix the heap-buffer-overflow described in the OpenEXR DWA decompression path, by making Huffman read bounds robust and avoiding out-of-bounds 4-byte reads.

1) What’s going wrong (root cause)
- The sanitizer reports a 4-byte read from a buffer that isn’t guaranteed to have 4 bytes left. In the problematic path, reads come from internal_huf.c using readUInt(b, end) with an end pointer that may not guarantee end-b >= 4 (or end could be behind b due to a miscomputed end).
- A robust fix is to ensure 4-byte reads only happen when there are at least 4 bytes available in the buffer, and to also guard against end < b (which could cause end-b to wrap and appear large).

2) Proposed fixes (2-3 approaches)
- Approach A (safe guard in readUInt): Change the readUInt implementation to explicitly check end >= b and that at least 4 bytes are available. If not, return 0. This guards every call site that uses the end-pointer variant.
- Approach B (use bounded read everywhere): Replace reads that pass an end-pointer with a bounded variant (readUIntBounded) everywhere (where feasible) so that 4-byte reads only occur if the end-pointer guarantees 4 bytes remain.
- Approach C (additional end checks at call sites): Add explicit end checks at call sites before invoking readUInt, in addition to the guard inside readUInt.

Most practical fix (minimally invasive, robust, and aligns with existing usage patterns):
- Implement the stronger check inside readUInt.
- Update call sites in internal_huf.c to use the bounded variant (readUIntBounded) wherever possible, so we get consistent boundary checks.

3) Minimal changes to implement

Patch A: Harden readUInt in internal_huf.c
- File: src/openexr/src/lib/OpenEXRCore/internal_huf.c

What to modify:
- Change the implementation of readUInt to guard against end < b and ensure at least 4 bytes are available.

Before:
static inline uint32_t
readUInt (const uint8_t* b, const uint8_t* end)
{
    if ((size_t)(end - b) >= 4) {
        return (
            ((uint32_t) b[0]) | (((uint32_t) b[1]) << 8u) |
            (((uint32_t) b[2]) << 16u) | (((uint32_t) b[3]) << 24u));
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
}

After:
static inline uint32_t
readUInt (const uint8_t* b, const uint8_t* end)
{
    // Ensure we don't read when end is before b or when fewer than 4 bytes remain
    if (end >= b && (size_t)(end - b) >= 4) {
        return (
            ((uint32_t) b[0]) | (((uint32_t) b[1]) << 8u) |
            (((uint32_t) b[2]) << 16u) | (((uint32_t) b[3]) << 24u));
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
}

Patch rationale:
- This guards against potential mis-specified end pointers (end < b) which could cause end - b to wrap to a large value and trigger a 4-byte read.
- It preserves the existing behavior when end-b >= 4.

Patch B: Use readUIntBounded at call sites in internal_huf.c
- File: src/openexr/src/lib/OpenEXRCore/internal_huf.c

Find and replace the three direct uses of readUInt with end-pointer:

- im = readUInt (compressed, ie);
- iM = readUInt (compressed + 4, ie);
- nBits = readUInt (compressed + 12, ie);

With bounded reads:
- im = readUIntBounded (compressed, ie);
- iM = readUIntBounded (compressed + 4, ie);
- nBits = readUIntBounded (compressed + 12, ie);

If you keep the original 4-byte-read version at any place, ensure it uses the same end-pointer logic as above. The bounded variant is already present in this file (static inline uint32_t readUIntBounded (const uint8_t* b, const uint8_t* end)), so this aligns with the existing pattern and helps prevent edge-case reads.

Patch C: Optional hardening in ImfHuf.cpp (defensive)
- File: src/openexr/src/lib/OpenEXR/ImfHuf.cpp

There is a readUIntBounded variant there as well (static inline unsigned int readUIntBounded (const char* p, const char* end)).
If you want additional defense, you can route:
- im = readUIntBounded (compressed, compressed + nCompressed);
- iM = readUIntBounded (compressed + 4, compressed + nCompressed);
- nBits = readUIntBounded (compressed + 12, compressed + nCompressed);

This mirrors the internal_huf.c approach and makes the path resilient to malformed input on the IMDWA/DWA path.

4) Verification steps (per the task)

- Build
  - Change directory to /src/openexr
  - Run secb build
  - Ensure there are no compile errors

- Reproduce
  - Run secb repro
  - The sanitizer should no longer report a heap-buffer-overflow in internal_huf.c readUInt when processing DWA-decompression input paths.

5) Rationale and trade-offs

- Why not only rely on readUIntBounded? It’s safer and aligns with the existing bounded path in internal_huf.c. It also reduces the risk of miscalculating end in the future.
- Why also harden readUInt? If end or end-end mismatch occurs anywhere, we still get a safe guard, preventing an accidental dereference in the event of a bug elsewhere causing end < b.
- Impact: The changes are localized to the Huffman code paths and do not alter the API surface or user-facing behavior for valid inputs. They primarily guard against malformed DWA input, which is the vulnerability described.

6) What you’ll see after applying

- internal_huf.c will guard against:
  -  end < b
  -  end - b < 4
- All 4-byte reads in Huffman decompression will only occur when at least 4 bytes are guaranteed to be available.
- If the input is malformed, readUInt returns 0 rather than reading out of bounds; the caller already checks the integrity via the existing logic (e.g., table sizes, end checks, etc.).

7) Notes for the reviewer

- If you prefer not to touch the public signatures, you can implement the guard only inside readUInt, but patching the call sites to readUIntBounded (as in Patch B) is strongly recommended to unify bounds handling and prevent similar issues in other code paths.
- Ensure any additional wrappers that call readUInt without end are updated to use the new guarded behavior.

If you want, I can provide exact diff hunks you can apply to your repo for Patch A and Patch B, or provide a combined patch script.

